---
// PROVENANCE MARKER, added 2026-09-03 by the Website thread, on his instruction: "the
// Services page was copy edited and streamlined, giving you a heads up, keep it and mark
// it". THE 2026-09-03 REWRITE BELOW IS NOT THIS LANE'S WORK. It arrived from a second
// session working the same repo while the Website lane was mid-sweep, and it is KEPT ON
// HIS WORD, unaltered.
//
// This lane checked it rather than taking it on trust, because it rewrote a page this
// lane had edited hours earlier: the false "source available, pure Python, no DRM" claim
// that came off on 2026-09-03 did NOT return, and the /pricing/ link that replaced the
// $2M commercial-licence line survived. Both verified against the built page.
//
// Two sessions committing to one repository is how a change gets silently reverted. It
// did not happen here. Anyone editing this page should check who else is in it first.
//
// Services page (Adrian x Claude Design, 2026-07-17; source drop:
// MrMiata inbox/Docs/"Services Docs.html"). The drop's copy matches the live page's
// approved copy in substance; em-dashes in the drop were NOT adopted (outbound-copy
// rule), its contractions were. The drop's decorative contact form is replaced by the
// real working ContactForm (Web3Forms), restyled to the drop's language.
// Shared furniture: designed-doc.css.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../../components/NavBar.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import ContactForm from '../../components/ContactForm.astro';
import Masthead from '../../components/Masthead.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import '../../styles/designed-doc.css';

// THE SERVICE CARD THUMBNAILS (Adrian, 2026-09-04). His renders, cut from one 1024x1024
// contact sheet he made: ConceptArt/FabricatorStudio/2026-09-01_services-cards/cards_page_v1.png,
// two columns by three rows, and he supplied the card-to-cell map himself. The sheet is
// UNTOUCHED where it sits; these are new files cut from a read of it.
//
// The sheet's rows are not equal thirds -- the gutters measure at y=396 and y=682, so the
// cells are 512x396, 512x285 and 512x342. Each was framed to a common 16:9 and resampled to
// 512x288, so six cards cannot render at six different heights.
//
// ONE CROP IS NOT THE FULL CELL, and it is his to overrule: the clay figure is a nude base
// mesh, and the full-height 16:9 window landed the bottom edge on its groin. It is cut
// waist-up instead, which still carries the wireframe arm that makes the render mean
// something. Say the word and it goes back to the full figure.
import thumbRigging from '../../assets/services/rigging-on-demand.png';
import thumbSupport from '../../assets/services/tools-support.png';
import thumbConsulting from '../../assets/services/production-consulting.png';
import thumbCustomTools from '../../assets/services/custom-tools-and-software.png';
import thumbRigDev from '../../assets/services/custom-rig-development.png';
import thumbTraining from '../../assets/services/team-training.png';

// THE SECOND SHEET (Adrian, 2026-09-04): cards_page2_v1.png in the same ConceptArt run, and
// again his own card-to-cell map. Three infographics for CUSTOM TOOLS and three for the
// HOW IT WORKS steps.
//
// Cut differently from the first sheet, for two measured reasons. The rows here are even
// thirds but the GUTTERS are wide (y 296-372 and 633-685, x 475-561), and splitting at the
// even boundary pulled a green speck of step three's checkmark into step one's tile; the
// bands are taken inside the gutters instead. And these are hard-edged objects rather than
// figures, so each is FIT into the 512x288 frame rather than cropped to fill it -- a cover
// crop sliced the top off the checkmark and the side off the app window. The plate is pure
// black, so the letterbox is invisible against it.
import thumbPipelines from '../../assets/services/pipelines.png';
import thumbCustomSoftware from '../../assets/services/custom-software.png';
import thumbOngoingHelp from '../../assets/services/ongoing-help.png';
import thumbStep1 from '../../assets/services/step-1-meeting.png';
import thumbStep2 from '../../assets/services/step-2-scope.png';
import thumbStep3 from '../../assets/services/step-3-delivery.png';


// CUT TO THE BONE, 2026-09-03. Adrian: "it's wayyyyyyy too long, and all over the place.
// Let's redo it to be very simple to understand. And it's not only pipelines but also
// custom software like Armature."
//
// What went: the four HOW I WORK value cards (transparency, agile, knowledge transfer,
// production quality), the Pipeline Health Audit block with its thirty-day credit, and the
// scope-and-pricing intro paragraph. Three sections became two. The promises worth keeping
// out of those cards - documented, readable, yours - are one clause in the lede now
// instead of four cards saying it four ways.
//
// CUSTOM SOFTWARE IS ITS OWN LINE, and Armature is the proof of it: a standalone app,
// built and shipped, sitting on this same site. That was the gap he named.
//
// The audit is not deleted from the record, only from the page: it was a real offer with a
// real credit against fix work, and putting it back is this array plus its markup from git.
// ===========================================================================================
// OUR SERVICES, added 2026-09-03 on his instruction, modelled on the section mcsGear runs
// for mGear. He named the six offerings; the copy below is DATA'S and he rules every word.
//
// WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT CARRIED OVER FROM THE mGear EXAMPLE, because none of it is
// true here and a services page is the worst place to find that out:
//   - "We". This is a one-person studio and the rest of the page says "I". He cut OUR from
//     the heading the same evening for that reason, so it is SERVICES.
//   - A private Slack channel, weekly meetings, a six-month minimum, recorded courses and
//     Pyblish validators. Those are mcsGear's terms and mcsGear's stack.
//   - A link per card. mcsGear's arrows go to six pages that exist. There are no such pages
//     here, so the cards do not promise one; Get in touch below is the single door.
//
// The claims that ARE made trace to something real: he is the author of Fabricator,
// Armature and FS Control Rig, all three of which are on this site.
// ===========================================================================================
// A COLOUR PER CARD (Adrian, 2026-09-03). RAISED WITH HIM: the DNA palette holds FOUR
// hues, plasma, cobalt, ember and magma, so six genuinely distinct colours do not exist in
// it. The first four cards take the four hues; the last two take the dim weights of plasma
// and cobalt, which read as related to their bright twins rather than as new colours.
// Adding hues five and six is a palette decision and his, not a CSS edit.
// scripts/check-brand-gates.mjs rejects any hex outside the set.
const services = [
  {
    accent: 'plasma',
    name: 'Rigging on demand',
    thumb: thumbRigging,
    body: 'Production rigs built to your skeleton and your standards, from one hero character to a full cast.',
  },
  {
    accent: 'cobalt',
    name: 'Tools support',
    thumb: thumbSupport,
    body: 'Priority support for studios running Fabricator, Armature or FS Control Rig in production, direct to the person who wrote them.',
  },
  {
    accent: 'ember',
    name: 'Production consulting',
    thumb: thumbConsulting,
    body: 'An audit of the pipeline you already have: tools, rigs, export, and where the time is actually going. You get a written report and an order to fix things in.',
  },
  {
    accent: 'magma',
    name: 'Custom tools and software',
    thumb: thumbCustomTools,
    body: 'Authoring tools, validators and standalone apps, built around how your team already works. Armature, on this site, is one of mine start to finish.',
  },
  {
    accent: 'plasma-dim',
    name: 'Custom rig development',
    thumb: thumbRigDev,
    body: 'Face and body rigs designed for the production rather than adapted from a template, with the reusable components your team builds on afterwards.',
  },
  {
    accent: 'cobalt-dim',
    name: 'Team training',
    thumb: thumbTraining,
    body: 'Sessions for your riggers, TDs and animators on your tools and your workflow, not a generic curriculum. Documentation and follow-up questions included.',
  },
];

// The line under the heading. DATA'S, and the one sentence on this page that tries to say
// what the whole offer is for.
const servicesCreed = 'One idea runs through all of it: you own what I build. The source, the documentation and the decisions stay with your team.';

const build = [
  {
    name: 'Pipelines',
    thumb: thumbPipelines,
    body: 'Rigging, skinning and export, wired end to end from Maya into your engine.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Custom software',
    thumb: thumbCustomSoftware,
    body: 'Standalone tools, when a script inside Maya is not the right shape for the job. Armature, the rigging app on this site, is one of mine.',
  },
  {
    // HIS LINE, 2026-09-03. It replaces "Fractional tech art, from a few days a month to
    // owning a slice of your pipeline." Two things raised with him rather than edited: the
    // name no longer describes the body, and fractional tech art was the only place on the
    // page that offer appeared.
    name: 'Ongoing help',
    thumb: thumbOngoingHelp,
    body: 'Every tool comes with full documentation and training built in.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Services | FabricatorStudio"
  description="Character pipelines and custom software, built to order by the technical artist behind Fabricator and Armature. Documented, and yours to keep."
>
  <NavBar />

  <article class="dd svc">
    {/* CUT TO THE MASTHEAD, 2026-09-03, on his instruction: the FABRICATORSTUDIO / SERVICES
        eyebrow, the tri-colour "Pipelines and software, built to order." title and the
        fourteen-years lede all come off, replaced by the same masthead /pricing/ and
        /account/ carry. His words for both lines. */}
    {/* The subtitle "Custom Pipelines and Software" came off within the hour (Adrian:
        "it's the same thing below basically"). WHAT I BUILD sits directly beneath and
        says it in three cards. */}
    <Masthead title="Services" />

    <!-- SERVICES: the menu of engagements. MOVED ABOVE CUSTOM TOOLS 2026-09-03 on his
         instruction, so the page opens on what can be bought rather than on what exists. -->
    <section class="svc-section" aria-labelledby="svc-services">
      <div class="svc-rule svc-rule--plasma">
        <h2 id="svc-services" class="svc-rule__label svc-rule__label--plasma">SERVICES</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="svc-creed">{servicesCreed}</p>
      <div class="svc-grid svc-grid--plasma">
        {services.map((v) => (
          <div class={`svc-card svc-card--${v.accent}`}>
            <h3 class="svc-card__name">{v.name}</h3>
            {/* BETWEEN THE TITLE AND THE PARAGRAPH (Adrian, 2026-09-04). Decorative on
                purpose: the h3 directly above already names the service, so an alt here
                would make a screen reader read every card's subject twice. */}
            <Image class="svc-card__thumb" src={v.thumb} alt="" widths={[320, 512]}
              sizes="(max-width: 700px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 42vw, 300px" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="svc-card__body">{v.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT I BUILD -->
    <section class="svc-section" aria-labelledby="svc-build">
      <div class="svc-rule svc-rule--cobalt">
        <h2 id="svc-build" class="svc-rule__label svc-rule__label--cobalt">CUSTOM TOOLS</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="svc-grid svc-grid--cobalt">
        {build.map((b) => (
          <div class="svc-card">
            <h3 class="svc-card__name">{b.name}</h3>
            <Image class="svc-card__thumb" src={b.thumb} alt="" widths={[320, 512]}
              sizes="(max-width: 700px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 42vw, 300px" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="svc-card__body">{b.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- HOW IT WORKS -->
    <section class="svc-section" aria-labelledby="svc-how">
      <div class="svc-rule svc-rule--ember">
        <h2 id="svc-how" class="svc-rule__label svc-rule__label--ember">HOW IT WORKS</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="svc-steps svc-steps--ember">
        <li class="svc-step">
          <div class="svc-step__head">
            <span class="svc-step__num svc-step__num--plasma" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
            <h3 class="svc-step__name">Mindmeld</h3>
          </div>
          {/* SAME PLACE AS THE CARDS (Adrian, 2026-09-04): under the heading element,
              above the paragraph. Here the heading element is the numeral. */}
          <Image class="svc-step__thumb" src={thumbStep1} alt="" widths={[320, 512]}
            sizes="(max-width: 700px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 42vw, 300px" loading="lazy" />
          {/* HIS LINE, 2026-09-03. MEETING carries the bold, holding the pattern that each
              step bolds the thing the client gets.
              TWO CHARACTERS CHANGED AND HE WAS TOLD. He wrote "and establish partnership".
              The sentence's verb is "Starts", so the second verb has to agree with it or
              the clause loses its subject; and "partnership" needs its article. */}
          <p class="svc-step__body">Starts with a <b>meeting</b> about your current bottlenecks, and establishes a partnership.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="svc-step">
          <div class="svc-step__head">
            <span class="svc-step__num svc-step__num--plasma" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
            <h3 class="svc-step__name">Blueprint</h3>
          </div>
<Image class="svc-step__thumb" src={thumbStep2} alt="" widths={[320, 512]}
            sizes="(max-width: 700px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 42vw, 300px" loading="lazy" />
          {/* HIS LINE, 2026-09-03, replacing "A written scope with deliverables and a price.
              Nothing starts until you have it." One character changed and he was told:
              he typed "your teams needs" and it is your TEAM'S needs, a possessive. */}
          <p class="svc-step__body">A <b>written scope</b> with deliverables. Adapted to your team&rsquo;s needs.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="svc-step svc-step--ember">
          <div class="svc-step__head">
            <span class="svc-step__num svc-step__num--ember" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
            <h3 class="svc-step__name">Delivery</h3>
          </div>
<Image class="svc-step__thumb" src={thumbStep3} alt="" widths={[320, 512]}
            sizes="(max-width: 700px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 42vw, 300px" loading="lazy" />
          {/* HIS LINE, 2026-09-03, replacing "Delivery in one focused burst, documented and
              handed over." The old one bolded a manner of working where steps 1 and 2 bold
              the thing the client receives, and "burst" read as rushed on a page selling
              pipeline work. DOCUMENTED IS BOLDED to hold that pattern: it is the artifact
              they keep. His to move.
              The second sentence is his too, added the same evening. Placed as written:
              "built" landing twice in one line is raised with him, not edited out. */}
          <p class="svc-step__body">Built to scope, <b>documented</b> and delivered. Team training and guidance built in.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- GET IN TOUCH -->
    <section class="svc-contact" aria-labelledby="svc-touch">
      <div class="svc-contact__copy">
        <h2 id="svc-touch" class="svc-contact__title">Get in touch</h2>
        <p class="svc-contact__sub">Tell me what's slowing your pipeline down. I'll tell you honestly whether I'm the right person to fix it, and what it would take.</p>
        <div class="svc-contact__alt">
          <span class="svc-contact__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p>Prefer email? <a href="mailto:adrian@fabricator.studio?subject=FabricatorStudio%20inquiry">adrian@fabricator.studio</a></p>
        </div>
        {/* The $2M commercial-licence line was cut 2026-09-03: 2.0 has no revenue
            threshold, so the sentence sold something that does not exist. */}
        <p class="svc-contact__lic">Seats for a team? Prices are on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="svc-contact__form">
        <ContactForm />
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The creed sits between the rule and the cards, held narrow so it reads as one thought
     rather than as a full-width paragraph competing with the grid under it. */
  .svc-creed {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    max-width: 62ch;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bone-dim);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* Page-specific: section rules, the card grids, the audit feature, the step cards, and
     the contact panel. Shared furniture: designed-doc.css. Drop's off-palette darks are
     snapped to tokens; hairline fades end at the hue at zero alpha, never `transparent`. */
  .svc .dd-header { align-items: center; text-align: center; }
  .svc-section { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; }
  .svc-rule { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.1rem; }
  .svc-rule__label {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.6vw, 1.625rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  /* THE TITLE TAKES ITS STRIPE'S COLOUR (Adrian, 2026-09-03). All three read bone before
     this, which is why the coloured rules looked unattached to the words beside them. */
  .svc-rule__label--plasma { color: var(--plasma); }
  .svc-rule__label--cobalt { color: var(--cobalt); }
  .svc-rule__label--ember { color: var(--ember); }
  .svc-rule::after { content: ''; flex: 1; height: 1px; }
  .svc-rule--plasma::after { background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.4), rgba(124, 255, 178, 0)); }
  .svc-rule--ember::after { background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255, 122, 61, 0.4), rgba(255, 122, 61, 0)); }
  /* A COLOUR PER SECTION (Adrian, 2026-09-03), so the three rules read as three places on
     the page rather than as one repeated ornament. Plasma and ember are the two functional
     accents; cobalt is the palette's structural tertiary and is what his own pricing design
     drop used for the non-featured card rails the same evening. Every hex here is in the
     set scripts/check-brand-gates.mjs enforces. THE LABELS ARE UNTOUCHED and stay bone:
     he asked for the line, not the heading. */
  .svc-rule--cobalt::after { background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(124, 184, 255, 0.4), rgba(124, 184, 255, 0)); }

  .svc-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1.25rem; }
  .svc-card {
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--iron), var(--carbon));
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 1.75rem 1.625rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  .svc-card--plasma { border-color: rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.14); }
  .svc-card--ember { border-color: rgba(255, 122, 61, 0.16); }
  .svc-card--wide { grid-column: span 2; }
  .svc-card__name { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--bone); }
  .svc-card__name--plasma { color: var(--bone); }
  .svc-card__name--ember { color: var(--bone); }
  /* THE THUMBNAIL (Adrian, 2026-09-04), between the name and the body. Inset rather than
     bled to the card edge: every one of these renders sits on a pure black plate, and a
     full-bleed black band against the card's iron-to-carbon gradient reads as a hole in the
     card rather than as a picture in it. The hairline gives it an edge to sit on.
     Aspect is fixed here as well as in the file, so a slow image cannot reflow the card. */
  .svc-card__thumb,
  .svc-step__thumb {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 10px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    margin: 0.15rem 0 0.25rem;
  }
  .svc-card__body { margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--bone-dim); }
  /* The numeral is 2.5rem of type and the card gap is 0.75rem; under a picture that reads
     as a gap twice over, so this one is pulled back up against its number. */
  .svc-step__thumb { margin-top: -0.15rem; }

  /* .svc-intro, .svc-audit* and the toned/wide card variants below are UNUSED as of
     2026-09-03: their markup came out in the copy cut. Kept, not deleted, because the
     audit was a real offer and restoring it is markup only while these rules stand. */
  .svc-intro { margin: -0.25rem 0 0; max-width: 52rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--bone-dim); text-wrap: pretty; }

  /* The featured audit: plasma-at-alpha wash over carbon (the guarantee card). */
  .svc-audit {
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.1), rgba(124, 255, 178, 0)), var(--carbon);
    border: 1px solid rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.4);
    border-radius: 20px;
    padding: 2rem 2.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    box-shadow: 0 0 44px -22px rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.4);
  }
  .svc-audit__head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .svc-audit__badge {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.8rem;
    border-radius: 7px;
    background: var(--plasma);
    color: #06120B;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  }
  .svc-audit__name { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.375rem; color: var(--bone); }
  .svc-audit__meta { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--bone-dim); }
  .svc-audit__body { margin: 0; max-width: 62rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--bone-dim); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .svc-audit__body :global(.b-plasma) { color: var(--bone); }

  .svc-steps { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1.25rem; }
  .svc-step {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--iron);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 1.625rem 1.5rem;
  }
  .svc-step--ember { border-color: rgba(255, 122, 61, 0.16); }

  /* A COLOURED EDGE PER SECTION (Adrian, 2026-09-03), following the section rules: plasma
     on Services, cobalt on Custom Tools, ember on How it works. Held at 0.3 alpha, which
     is the weight his pricing drop used for the featured card's border. Any harder and six
     outlined boxes stop reading as cards and start reading as warnings. Every hue is in the
     set scripts/check-brand-gates.mjs enforces. */
  .svc-grid--plasma .svc-card { border-color: rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.3); }
  /* Per-card overrides, one hue each. Same 0.3 weight as the section edges. */
  .svc-card--plasma { border-color: rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.3) !important; }
  .svc-card--cobalt { border-color: rgba(124, 184, 255, 0.3) !important; }
  .svc-card--ember { border-color: rgba(255, 122, 61, 0.3) !important; }
  .svc-card--magma { border-color: rgba(255, 59, 59, 0.3) !important; }
  .svc-card--plasma-dim { border-color: rgba(79, 184, 136, 0.45) !important; }
  .svc-card--cobalt-dim { border-color: rgba(79, 132, 190, 0.45) !important; }
  .svc-grid--cobalt .svc-card { border-color: rgba(124, 184, 255, 0.3); }
  .svc-steps--ember .svc-step { border-color: rgba(255, 122, 61, 0.3); }
  /* THE NUMERAL AND THE NAME SHARE A ROW (Adrian, 2026-09-04). Baseline-aligned, not
     centred: the numeral is 2.5rem against the name's 1.125rem, and centring them makes the
     name float in the middle of the digit rather than stand on the same line. */
  .svc-step__head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.7rem; }
  .svc-step__num { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 800; font-size: 2.5rem; line-height: 1; }
  .svc-step__name { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--bone); }
  .svc-step__num--plasma { color: var(--bone); -webkit-text-stroke: 1px rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.4); }
  .svc-step__num--ember { color: var(--bone); -webkit-text-stroke: 1px rgba(255, 122, 61, 0.4); }
  .svc-step__body { margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--bone-dim); }
